div></div></div></div></div></div><div data-test-render-count="2"><div class="group" style="height: auto; opacity: 1; transform: none;"><div class="contents"><div data-is-streaming="false" class="group relative relative pb-3" style="opacity: 1; transform: none;"><div class="font-claude-response relative leading-[1.65rem] [&amp;_pre&gt;div]:bg-bg-000/50 [&amp;_pre&gt;div]:border-0.5 [&amp;_pre&gt;div]:border-border-400 [&amp;_.ignore-pre-bg&gt;div]:bg-transparent [&amp;_.standard-markdown_:is(p,blockquote,h1,h2,h3,h4,h5,h6)]:pl-2 [&amp;_.standard-markdown_:is(p,blockquote,ul,ol,h1,h2,h3,h4,h5,h6)]:pr-8 [&amp;_.progressive-markdown_:is(p,blockquote,h1,h2,h3,h4,h5,h6)]:pl-2 [&amp;_.progressive-markdown_:is(p,blockquote,ul,ol,h1,h2,h3,h4,h5,h6)]:pr-8"><div><div class="standard-markdown grid-cols-1 grid [&amp;_&gt;_*]:min-w-0 gap-3 standard-markdown"><p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">Angular momentum is the rotational analog of linear momentum â€” a measure of how much "spin" or rotational motion an object has.</p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]"><strong>The basics</strong></p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">For a point mass moving in a circle, angular momentum <strong>L</strong> is defined as:</p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]"><strong>L</strong> = <strong>r</strong> Ã— <strong>p</strong> = <strong>r</strong> Ã— m<strong>v</strong></p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">where <strong>r</strong> is the position vector from the axis of rotation and <strong>p</strong> = m<strong>v</strong> is the linear momentum. The magnitude simplifies to L = mvr sin Î¸, or just L = mvr when the velocity is perpendicular to the radius.</p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">For a rigid body rotating about a fixed axis:</p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]"><strong>L</strong> = I<strong>Ï‰</strong></p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">where I is the moment of inertia and <strong>Ï‰</strong> is the angular velocity. This mirrors Newton's second law analog: <strong>Ï„</strong> = dL/dt (torque equals the rate of change of angular momentum).</p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]"><strong>Conservation</strong></p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">Angular momentum is conserved when the net external torque on a system is zero. This is one of the most powerful principles in physics:</p>
<ul class="[li_&amp;]:mb-0 [li_&amp;]:mt-1 [li_&amp;]:gap-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc flex flex-col gap-1 pl-8 mb-3">
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">A figure skater pulls in their arms â†’ smaller I â†’ larger Ï‰ (spins faster)</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">Planets sweep equal areas in equal times (Kepler's second law)</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">A gyroscope resists changes in orientation</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">Neutron stars spin incredibly fast because a collapsing star conserves L while I shrinks dramatically</li>
</ul>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]"><strong>Quantization</strong></p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">In quantum mechanics, angular momentum is quantized. Orbital angular momentum takes values L = â„âˆš(â„“(â„“+1)) for integer â„“, and particles also carry intrinsic spin (e.g., electrons have spin-Â½). This quantization is fundamental to atomic structure and the periodic table.</p>
