<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]"><strong>Heatmap (what we have now)</strong></p>
<ul class="[li_&amp;]:mb-0 [li_&amp;]:mt-1 [li_&amp;]:gap-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc flex flex-col gap-1 pl-8 mb-3">
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">Instantly shows the magnitude and sign of anomalies across all latitudes at a glance</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">The color gradient makes it easy to spot the tropical westerly bursts and poleward propagation patterns</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">Very close to what the NOAA PSL reference image you shared uses (shading + contours overlaid)</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">Can look "muddy" if the data is noisy or the color scale isn't well tuned</li>
</ul>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]"><strong>Contours only</strong></p>
<ul class="[li_&amp;]:mb-0 [li_&amp;]:mt-1 [li_&amp;]:gap-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc flex flex-col gap-1 pl-8 mb-3">
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">Cleaner, more classic meteorological look â€” exactly like the black-and-white CPC/PSL diagnostic plots</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">Better for showing the <em>structure</em> and propagation of anomalies (you can trace the lines)</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">Easier to read specific Ïƒ values precisely</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">Loses the immediate visual "punch" of color â€” positive and negative regions aren't instantly obvious without reading the labels</li>
</ul>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]"><strong>The reference image you shared</strong> actually uses both together â€” shaded color for anomalies and solid contour lines for the total field on top. That hybrid approach is arguably the best of both worlds and is the meteorological standard for this type of diagram.</p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">My recommendation would be to <strong>keep the heatmap + contour overlay</strong> as currently designed, but I'd suggest one tweak: use a <strong>diverging blue-white-red colorscale clipped to Â±1.5Ïƒ</strong> instead of Â±2Ïƒ. That tighter range makes the tropical signal pop more and reduces the washed-out look in the extratropics where variance is higher.</p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">That said â€” do you have a strong preference for one style? Or would you like me to build a toggle button into the viewer so you can switch between heatmap+contours, contours-only, and heatmap-only on the fly?</p></div></div></div></div></div><div class="flex justify-start opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 group-focus-within:opacity-100 transition" role="group" aria-label="Message actions"><div class="text-text-300"><div class="text-text-300 flex items-stretch justify-between"><div class="w-fit" data-state="closed"><button class="inline-flex
